  "title": "Walk the fox",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "md": "## A call is a step\n\nA call is one step the fox takes. `Player.move(\"east\")` walks a single cell in the direction you name. East increases **x**, south increases **y**. The beacon never comes to you; you walk onto it.\n\nThis bites when you expect one call to cover the whole distance, or when you mix the axes and walk north where you meant south. From `(0, 0)`, one east call leaves the fox at `(1, 0)` — not at the origin, and not already on a far beacon.\n\n```python\nPlayer.move(\"east\")\nPlayer.move(\"east\")\nPlayer.move(\"east\")\nPlayer.move(\"south\")\nPlayer.move(\"south\")\n```\n\nA common mistake is passing coordinates instead of a direction, or stopping after one step. The exercise asks you to walk from `(0, 0)` onto the beacon at `(3, 2)`: three east calls and two south calls, in any order."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "id": "where-after-east",
      "promptMd": "The fox starts at `(0, 0)`. After one `Player.move(\"east\")`, where is it?",
      "kind": "mcq",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "same",
          "md": "Still at `(0, 0)`"
        },
        {
          "id": "east",
          "md": "At `(1, 0)`"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "east",
      "explainMd": "One call is one cell. East adds 1 to x, so the fox lands on `(1, 0)` and waits there for the next call. Nothing moves the fox except a call you wrote, and no single call ever covers the whole distance."
    },
